.TH POPLAB 2
.SH NAME
poplab: run, parse \- Danby population models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "poplab.m";
poplab := load Poplab Poplab->PATH;

err := poplab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, err) := poplab->parse(spec);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Poplab
has the same relationship to the Danby population viewer that
.IR mechlab (2)
has to the mechanism one: the viewer as a library the model calls,
driven by a script, rather than a command that loads the model back
through a module interface.  A model is one file:
.PP
.EX
poplab->run(ctxt, "budworm", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
The vocabulary is
.IR dynsys (2)'s
\(em
.BR param ,
.BR maxtime ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.B eq
\(em plus
.B title
and
.BR manual ,
which mean what they mean in
.IR mechlab (2).
.PP
There is no geometry here and no pair of observables.  Every state is
plotted and given its own bar, so a state's name is its label, and a
parameter's slider runs from zero to its maximum, which is what this
family's own
.B parameterranges()
meant.
.PP
.B Maxtime
is not used by the viewer \(em a population model runs until it is
stopped \(em but
.IR dynsys (2)
requires one; say any positive number.
.PP
The window is Tk's.  The parameter sliders are Tk scales, the buttons are
Tk buttons, the readout is a Tk label, and the layout is a Tk geometry
manager's; the history plot and the state bars are two
.IR panel (9)
widgets holding images the library draws into.  A click on a bar arrives
as a Tk binding with coordinates already relative to that panel, so
setting a state by hand costs one division by the panel's own height.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/poplab.b
